<header>
  <div class="eyebrow">Philosophy • Law • Justice</div>
  <h1>Justice and the Death Penalty</h1>
  <p>An examination of retribution, deterrence, and the question of whether capital punishment satisfies the demands of justice.</p>
</header>

<main>
<article>

<blockquote>
  “Whoever has committed murder, must die. There is, in this case, no juridical substitute or surrogate that can be given to satisfy the requirements of justice.”
  <cite>— Immanuel Kant, <em>The Metaphysics of Morals</em></cite>
</blockquote>

<p class="lead">
The death penalty has been debated for centuries, but one of the central questions is whether taking the life of a murderer is necessary to satisfy justice. Immanuel Kant argued that when someone commits murder, death is the appropriate punishment because no lesser punishment can satisfy the requirements of justice. His argument is based on <strong>retribution</strong>—the idea that a punishment should be proportionate to the crime committed.
</p>

<h2>The Death Penalty in the United States</h2>

<p>
In the United States, the death penalty is a state-sanctioned punishment in which a person convicted of a qualifying crime is executed. Cornell Law School explains that the Supreme Court has determined that the death penalty is not automatically prohibited by the Eighth Amendment, although the Constitution places restrictions on how and when it can be imposed.
</p>

<p>
The Supreme Court's treatment of the death penalty has changed over time. In <em>Furman v. Georgia</em> (1972), the Court ruled that the death penalty was unconstitutional when it was applied in an arbitrary or discriminatory manner. Later, in <em>Gregg v. Georgia</em> (1976), the Court held that the death penalty itself was not necessarily unconstitutional and recognized retribution and deterrence as purposes that could justify capital punishment.
</p>

<h2>Retribution and Justice</h2>

<p>
This connects directly to Kant's argument. If justice requires a punishment proportionate to the seriousness of murder, then the death penalty can be viewed as fulfilling a retributive purpose. However, the question becomes more complicated when considering whether capital punishment actually provides a greater deterrent against murder.
</p>

<h2>Does the Death Penalty Deter Murder?</h2>

<p>
The National Academies examined research concerning whether the death penalty deters homicide. After reviewing decades of research, the committee concluded that the existing research could not reliably establish whether capital punishment decreases homicide rates, increases them, or has no effect on them. In other words, the available research did not provide a reliable answer to the deterrence question.
</p>

<p>
This distinction is important. <strong>Retribution and deterrence are different arguments.</strong> Kant's quotation is an argument about justice and the punishment deserved for murder. Deterrence, by contrast, asks whether the existence or use of the death penalty prevents other people from committing murders. The National Academies' findings indicate that the research available to its committee could not establish a clear relationship between capital punishment and homicide rates.
</p>

<h2>The Strongest Argument for Capital Punishment</h2>

<p>
Therefore, the strongest argument for the death penalty based on these sources is not necessarily that it has been proven to prevent murder. Instead, the argument can be made on the principle of justice: that a person who deliberately takes another person's life may deserve the ultimate punishment. This is the principle expressed by Kant when he argues that no substitute punishment can satisfy the requirements of justice.
</p>

<h2>Constitutional Limits</h2>

<p>
At the same time, the legal system does not treat every murder as automatically deserving death. Cornell Law School explains that capital sentencing requires consideration of the particular circumstances of the crime and the defendant, and the Supreme Court has placed constitutional limits on who may receive a death sentence.
</p>

<div class="question">
  “What punishment does justice require, and does capital punishment actually deter murder?”
</div>

<p>
The death penalty therefore raises two different questions: <strong>What punishment does justice require, and does capital punishment actually deter murder?</strong> Kant provides a philosophical argument for the first question, while the National Academies' research addresses the second. The evidence concerning deterrence does not provide a definite answer, leaving the question of whether the death penalty is justified partly dependent on one's understanding of justice and punishment.
</p>

